title: Bonding & Structure
subject: Chemistry
level: GCSE
emoji: 🧪
description: Ionic, covalent and metallic bonding, plus giant structures.
---
What is ionic bonding? :: Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions formed by transfer of electrons.
What is covalent bonding? :: A shared pair of electrons between two non-metal atoms.
What is metallic bonding? :: Attraction between positive metal ions and a sea of delocalised electrons.
Which types of elements form ionic compounds? :: A metal with a non-metal.
Why do ionic compounds have high melting points? :: Strong electrostatic forces between ions need lots of energy to overcome.
When can ionic compounds conduct electricity? :: When molten or dissolved – the ions are free to move.
Why do simple molecular substances have low melting points? :: Weak intermolecular forces are broken, not the strong covalent bonds.
Why can't simple molecules conduct electricity? :: They have no overall charge and no free charged particles.
Describe the structure of diamond. :: Each carbon is bonded to four others in a rigid giant covalent lattice – very hard.
Why does graphite conduct electricity? :: Each carbon bonds to three others, leaving one delocalised electron per atom.
What is graphene? :: A single layer of graphite, one atom thick.
What is a fullerene? :: A hollow-shaped carbon molecule, e.g. buckminsterfullerene C₆₀.
Why are metals malleable? :: Layers of ions can slide over each other.
Why do metals conduct electricity and heat? :: Delocalised electrons carry charge and energy.
What is an alloy? :: A mixture of a metal with another element; harder than pure metals as layers can't slide easily.
What charge does a Group 1 ion have? :: 1+
What charge does a Group 2 ion have? :: 2+
What charge does a Group 7 ion have? :: 1−
What is the formula of magnesium oxide? :: MgO
What is the formula of calcium chloride? :: CaCl₂
How many covalent bonds does carbon usually form? :: Four.
What is a nanoparticle? :: A particle 1–100 nm in size, with a very high surface-area-to-volume ratio.
